WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.180
They met in the dissection hall at Prague and recognized each other at once as the

2
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.380
same kind of person.

3
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:17.993
They married, came to the United States in 1922, and ran headlong into the rules of

4
00:00:17.993 --> 00:00:23.053
the era: a Buffalo institute director ordered Gerty to stop publishing with her

5
00:00:23.053 --> 00:00:29.093
husband, and she refused; at Washington University she was hired at a tenth of

6
00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:32.753
Carl's salary for sixteen years under nepotism rules.

7
00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:39.603
The lab she built at that salary trained five future Nobel laureates.

8
00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:45.683
Working through a fraction everyone else threw away the supernatant left from

9
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:52.143
recrystallizing phosphorylase Gerty and a young colleague, Joseph Larner, found the

10
00:00:52.143 --> 00:00:58.103
debranching enzyme, and Gerty understood the chromatography spot instantly and ran

11
00:00:58.103 --> 00:01:02.063
down the hall shouting, "It's free glucose, it's free glucose!"

12
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:09.237
She went further, matching the glycogen storage disease in sick children to a

13
00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:15.617
specific missing enzyme: the first proof that one broken enzyme can cause an

14
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.737
inherited disease.

15
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:25.130
When the Nobel came in 1947, Carl said at the banquet that including his wife was "a

16
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.370
source of deep satisfaction to me."

17
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.870
He was not being gallant; he was being accurate.

18
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.290
The same year, Washington University finally made Gerty a full professor.
